00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
welcome back everyone 6  38 and metro sheriff joe lombardo  joining us now for today's morning cup  of joe great to see you sheriff good  morning  good morning alex how are you good hope  you're doing well a lot to get to here  in this segment we want to start with  that officer-involved shooting from over  the weekend  it was reported the officers were being  shot at in that incident can you tell us  a bit more about what happened and how  those officers are doing  sure i mean obviously it's still under  investigation so i could give you some  limited information  but we were called to the scene by the  female  person reporting we call him pr she was  concerned for a significant other he had  been drinking  a large amount of alcohol and making  you know aggressive statements i would  leave it at that  and upon our arrival we were able to  have the  family members exit the residence and  they informed us he was in possession of  some  firearms and he was  under out of control i would use that  term  so subsequently we responded and i'm  very proud of our officers because  the initial response was de-escalation  so  we surrounded attempt to make contact  with the individual  and he as you can see by the video he  came outside with a firearm  and he did fire upon the police officer  on several occasions  and then subsequently we returned one  shot  to the individual and i understand he  had a grazing wound from our shot  and eventually we were able to talk him  out of it so he exited the residence to  try to uh  jump over a wall in the rear of the  residence and we took them into custody  without further incidents so those are  the type of things we deal with on a  regular basis and  usually the public's not aware of that  those kind of  barricaded incidents are very common in  our valley  are yeah and that's chilling video to  watch sheriff  um another issue we've been talking  about it seems like for a few months now  a major spike in stolen cars up about 30  percent from this time last year  are you seeing this happening in a  certain part of town  yeah we are seeing it happening it's  closer to i think it's 28  in today's numbers um and it is not in  particular  to a certain part of town so it's  occurring throughout the town so  um that's unfortunate for us because we  can't put our  thumb on it you know in the short term  versus the long term but we are getting  a handle on that and i think the most  important piece on this is public  education  and awareness so the most common  form of theft is people leaving their  vehicle running and unattended so  such as going into a convenience store  to get a cup of coffee  or whatever they may purchase and then  people take advantage of that so  the most important piece is to be aware  of your surroundings  park in well-lit areas do not leave your  your garage door openers or other  valuable items within the vehicle and do  the best you can to make it harder for  individuals to steal your vehicle  and and the motivation behind it um  you know we can theorize that until the  cows come home but the  most important thing is is you know  people are going back to work  uh the availability of cars is lessening  and so  you're looking for transportation both  to and from  their heights it's very difficult  to put arms around but we are aware of  it  pressing it we're doing the best we can  sheriff governor sysolak signed two  police reform bills into law yesterday  the first one allows the attorney  general's office to conduct pattern and  practice investigations  and the piece of the other one limits no  knock warrants what are your thoughts on  those two  you know and i i got to be uh frank with  you on there  they're fantastic bills i i we worked  with the attorney general on those bills  but they're sort of benign to lbmpd  and the reason why i say that is i'm not  concerned about a pattern practice  investigation within my own police  department but  the need for the attorney general to  have that available to them is very  important  so we can determine whether there are  racist types actions conducted by police  departments or there's individuals or  even as a group they have to be able to  investigate that and that bill gives  them the authority to do that  and the cooperation is necessary  between the police departments and  attorney general  the other one was the no knock warrant  restrictions and that's where you have  a police agency or a group or a unit  conduct a search warrant on a location  and they don't knock at the door to give  warning to the residents or the  occupants  and they eventually use force to gain  entry um  they basically adopted our policy our  existing policy  with some negotiation work  warning we had that in place um  we haven't had any um incidents that  required us to  look at that in the recent history and  long history  but i think it's important for people to  know that that was a negotiation between  attorney general and local law  enforcement  and be coming to a consensus and what  would be better for the community  and protection to the community to  ensure we don't have an  unnecessary death governor you  sheriff rather you spoke recently about  a possible run for governor in 2022  you since confirmed that with the tv  station in reno earlier this week can  you confirm that with us  and why are you running you know i i can  confirm that with you  but unfortunately we  i kind of got caught off a little off  guard  up there in reno but uh you know that's  i gotta be frank with the public and  and and i am running for governor um we  plan on a formal announcement here in  the next coming weeks  and i will um display or provide my  platform uh moving forward at that time  we look forward to talking to you about  that then sheriff thank you so much for  joining us this morning hope you have a  great week  thank you alex if you have a question  for the sheriff you can send it to us on  our website  newsnow.com and find out if your  question was answered  every wednesday during morning cup of  joe i'll

